my name is olivia durant and i had no  idea what i looked like until just a few  years ago i was blind from birth until  my 30s and this is that story so i was  born with severe severe severe myopia i  could see incredible detail from the tip  of my nose to my face but from the tip  of my nose onward i could see nothing  all i could see was shape color floaters  flashers  sort of like a kaleidoscope i didn't  come from a family that had money and i  actually was abandoned by my parents as  a child so i ended up getting thrown  into public school with sighted kids i  did not get to go to the school for the  blind and i didn't learn braille i  didn't have any accommodation other than  maybe get up to the front of the room  and try so i would take a book and i  would smash it against my face and i  would read like this and i could see the  paper fibers i could see the ink but i  would put together the letters into  words and just go like this and i  actually would have ink on the tip of my  nose from  smearing it looked weird i just look  like the stereotypical giant glasses  nerd on steroids i was told that i was  stupid fat and ugly for my entire life  by some of my family members but also by  most of the kids i went to school with  because that's i guess what bullies do i  couldn't see myself to really sort out  that that was wrong so i was unable to  really establish a sense of self and i  took on this sense of self that other  people told me i should have i was  getting bullied and beaten up so much at  school like i would just stand there not  be able to see anything somebody would  grab me next thing i know i'd be on the  floor and some boy would be kicking me  in the side or or spitting on me  something like that and they just let  this happen because i guess there's too  many kids and there's not enough  teachers right so i joined a karate  school and the guy that owned the school  ended up being like a second father to  me i was pretty terrible at it but i  would just sit up there with him and  read books or talk and it was a place  where i felt that i had a family and  he's like well i'll teach you  so i ended up learning how to fight  there so from you know if somebody  grabbed my arm i knew how to defend  against it and i started fighting  semi-professionally and tables started  turning it gave me confidence that i did  not have been a family that i didn't  have as well so i it was a great point  in my life surgery existed to give me  sight in the 90s  and they i remember one did mention if  we replace the lens of your eye with an  implant just like cataract surgery  you'll be able to see and at the time i  think it was something like forty  thousand dollars and uh they were just  like yeah we're not gonna recommend that  to you because it's just too expensive  and insurance doesn't cover it  so what happened was in 2014 i developed  actual cataracts and then they were like  well the insurance is going to cover it  but you need a laser surgery on top of  that so that's going to be about ten  thousand dollars extra and i said  something about it on facebook and in  three hours  uh people that knew me had donated over  ten thousand dollars and i said please  stop i want to give the rest to charity  i ended up going in for four surgeries  you have to look at a laser and the  laser divides your eye  into a checkerboard shape and then they  implant and inject a lens that unfurls  into your eye the whole process took  from january 2016 until about november  2016. it wasn't the easiest thing i've  ever done so i remember when i woke up  from surgery it wasn't like a big bang  thing right where you just wake up and  like i can see perfectly when i was able  to see people it was like  too much information  i developed really bad anxiety because  it's like if you've ever seen any of  those sci-fi shows where some normal  person gets psychic powers and then they  have a nervous breakdown and starts  screaming that's what happened to me  and doctors are medical doctors they're  not going to say oh be prepared this  might put you in therapy  but apparently  most people it does i get along really  well with transgender people because  they get the idea of dramatic rebirth  that you can't ignore and you have to  deal emotionally and mentally but it was  very hard i would say the hardest years  of my life  were the two years after my surgery  so i went into this salad bar place  right after surgery and it had one of  those mirrored walls  and i walked in and i saw a person next  to me and it's that awkward thing where  somebody is so close to you that you  want you have to say hi to them right  and i was like oh hi and  i said hello to myself  and then i'm like looking did anybody  see me say hi to myself the reflection  in the mirror and i'm like no and then  then i was like oh my god that's me do i  look like that oh wow what and i just  started crying i had a nervous breakdown  and i probably looked like a person that  was losing her grip on reality but i  didn't recognize  myself at all and even sometimes at  night now i will look at myself in the  mirror and be like is  that me  and if i'm not paying attention  i can revert to  the person that i was told i was before  i got the surgery because i feel like  everybody is programmed at a young age  with their identity my life has been  hard but  i'm actually really happy and grateful  for it because  i was able to get through it and i was  able to  be stronger afterwards and  the main thing that i want to do is i  want to make a difference on the planet  for people that feel that maybe their  disability or their situation is  a lost cause it's not  like your disability is where your  superpower is  and even before i had my eye surgery my  disability was where my superpower is  because i looked at myself closer and i  was able to realize  how strong i was and i'm not special  everyone has it in them  [Music]  you
 

